  "text": "Assistant: To find the value of the determinant:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nwe can use the property that the determinant of a matrix with symmetric elements can be simplified. Specifically, the determinant of a \\(2 \\times 2\\) matrix with rows that are scalar multiples of each other is zero. However, in this case, we have a \\(3 \\times 3\\) matrix where each row is a scalar multiple of the row above it due to the trigonometric relationships.\n\nLet's consider the first, second, and third rows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe can observe that the third column is a linear combination of the first and second columns. To confirm this, let's write out the determinant in a step-by-step manner using properties of determinants and trigonometric identities.\n\nFirst, let's express the determinant in a factored form:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix} = \\cos^2 \\alpha \\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix} - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha \\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix} + \\sin^2 \\alpha \\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's compute each of these \\(2 \\times 2\\) determinants:\n\n1. \\(\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix} = \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta \\sin^2 \\gamma - \\sin^2 \\beta \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma = \\sin \\beta \\sin \\gamma (\\cos \\beta \\sin \\gamma - \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\beta) = \\sin \\beta \\sin \\gamma \\sin (2 \\beta - 2 \\gamma)\\)\n\n2. \\(\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix} = \\cos^2 \\beta \\sin^2 \\gamma - \\sin^2 \\beta \\cos^2 \\gamma = \\cos^2 \\beta \\sin^2 \\gamma - \\sin^2 \\beta \\cos^2 \\gamma = \\cos^2 \\beta \\sin^2 \\gamma - \\sin^2 \\beta \\cos^2 \\gamma\\)\n\n3. \\(\\begin{vmatrix} \n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta \\\\ \n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma \n\\end{vmatrix} = \\cos^2 \\beta \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma - \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta \\cos^2 \\gamma = \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta (\\cos \\beta \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma - \\cos^2 \\gamma)\\)\n\nPutting it all together, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos^2 \\alpha (\\sin \\beta \\sin \\gamma \\sin (2 \\beta - 2 \\gamma)) - \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha (\\cos^2 \\beta \\sin^2 \\gamma - \\sin^2 \\beta \\cos^2 \\gamma) + \\sin^2 \\alpha \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta (\\cos \\beta \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma - \\cos^2 \\gamma)\n\\]\n\nSince the angles \\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\beta\\), and \\(\\gamma\\) satisfy \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\), we can use trigonometric identities to simplify the expression. However, a more straightforward approach is to recognize that the determinant of the original matrix is zero due to the symmetry and the fact that the rows are linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the value of the determinant is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
